    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Differential diagnosis of classic Hodgkin lymphoma (CHL) by cytopathology</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Mousa A. Al-Abbadi",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 658.8pt; border-collapse: collapse;\" border=\"0\" width=\"878\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"break-inside: avoid; height: 3.5pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 140.1pt; border-right: none; border-bottom: none; border-left: none; border-image: initial; border-top: 1pt solid windowtext; background: #8db3e2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"187\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; break-after: avoid;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Major differential diagnosis of CHL</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8cm; border-right: none; border-bottom: none; border-left: none; border-image: initial; border-top: 1pt solid windowtext; background: #8db3e2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; break-after: avoid;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Cytopathological clues to help make the distinction</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 291.9pt; border-right: none; border-bottom: none; border-left: none; border-image: initial; border-top: 1pt solid windowtext; background: #8db3e2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt; height: 3.5pt;\" width=\"389\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; break-after: avoid;\"><strong style=\"font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif; font-size: 12pt;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Immunophenotypic features by&nbsp;</span></strong><span style=\"font-family: Times New Roman, serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\"><strong>immunocytochemistry</strong></span></span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 140.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"187\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Reactive lymphadenopathies with increased numbers of atypical large immunoblasts, such as infectious mononucleosis and other viral infections</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8cm; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">In reactive lymphadenopathies, a spectrum of background lymphocytes and large lymphoid cells is usually noted, and tingible-body macrophages are often prominent. Immunocytochemistry&nbsp;will resolve the differential diagnosis (see ancillary testing details).</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 291.9pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"389\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">In reactive lymphadenopathies, the large cells express CD45 and many mature B-cell markers (e.g. CD20, CD19, and PAX5). In CHL, the HCs and RSCs lack CD45 (LCA) and CD20 in most cases. PAX5 will show characteristic weaker staining than surrounding mature B-cells in CHL, a very helpful feature of HRS cells. CD15 and CD30 are usually positive in the majority of the HCs and RSCs in CHL, and negative in immunoblasts and other mimics.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 140.1pt; background: #daeef3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"187\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN-GB\">T-cell/histiocyte&ndash;rich large B-cell lymphoma (THRLBCL)</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8cm; background: #daeef3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">THRLBCL can mimic CHL when occurring in lymph nodes, especially when large atypical pleomorphic cells are present, and it may be very difficult to make the distinction from NLPHL. Immunocytochemistry&nbsp;is usually needed.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 291.9pt; background: #daeef3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"389\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Almost all LBCLs express several B-cell markers (e.g. CD20, CD79a), as well as CD45, which are usually negative in the RSCs and HCs of CHL.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 140.1pt; border-top: none; border-right: none; border-left: none; border-image: initial; border-bottom: 1pt solid windowtext; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"187\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><strong><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Anaplastic large cell lymphoma (ALCL)</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8cm; border-top: none; border-right: none; border-left: none; border-image: initial; border-bottom: 1pt solid windowtext; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">ALCL may contain a background of polymorphous lymphoid cells in addition to the large neoplastic cells, but the presence of numerous large atypical cells favours ALCL.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 291.9pt; border-top: none; border-right: none; border-left: none; border-image: initial; border-bottom: 1pt solid windowtext; padding: 0cm 5.4pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"389\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\" style=\"margin: 3pt 0cm; font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">The large atypical cells of ALK-positive ALCL express ALK and/or multiple T-cell markers, such as CD2, CD3, and CD5. B-cell markers, such as CD20, CD79a, and PAX5, are usually negative in ALCL. CD30 expression is positive in both and is therefore not helpful in this distinction.</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p><span lang=\"EN-GB\" style=\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', serif;\">HC, Hodgkin cell; HRS cell, Hodgkin/Reed&ndash;Sternberg cell; LBCL, large B-cell lymphoma; NLPHL, nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma; RSC, Reed&ndash;Sternberg cell.</span></p>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  }
]